<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <!-- Required so live weather/routing fetches work in RELEASE builds.
         (debug/profile manifests declare INTERNET separately and merge it
         into those variants; release uses only this main manifest.) -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET"/>
    <!-- Foreground-only location: shows the driver's live position dot on the snow map
         while driving. NO ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION by design (the app is
         foreground-only; background tracking would be a dignity over-reach). -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION"/>
    <!-- WS5 actuator channel: WAKE_LOCK holds the screen on so a driver
         glancing at a live hazard never finds a dark screen (wakelock_plus). -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.WAKE_LOCK"/>
    <!-- VIBRATE: the haptic cue she feels WITHOUT LOOKING — the one alert
         channel that survives a whiteout and a screen she cannot glance at
         (the alert must reach a sense she has free). Driven by the `vibration` plugin at
         lib/actuators/mobile_alert_actuators.dart:191-192.

         DECLARED HERE 2026-08-10. It was ALREADY SHIPPING — the plugin
         injected it at manifest-merge time — but it was absent from this file,
         the file humans read when they write declarations. That gap is not
         cosmetic: the public privacy policy was written from this manifest,
         listed four permissions, stated "no other permissions are requested",
         and was FALSE for a month against an app that requests five. A
         permission we ship must be visible in the source we author, or the next
         declaration written from it is wrong again. Gate 5 of
         tool/preflight_play_upload.sh now compares this list against the built
         artifact so the drift cannot recur silently. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.VIBRATE"/>
    <!-- Foreground-service perms RETURNED 2026-09-24, on the exact condition
         the 2026-07-10 removal set: "when the explicit, user-visible,
         driver-initiated ongoing-drive Service is actually built,
         FOREGROUND_SERVICE + FOREGROUND_SERVICE_LOCATION return in the same
         change-set." They do, and this is that change-set.

         The Service is NOT newly written and NOT ours: it is
         com.baseflow.geolocator.GeolocatorLocationService, already declared
         with android:foregroundServiceType="location" in geolocator_android's
         own manifest and already merged into our APK (verified in
         build/app/intermediates/merged_manifests/release/.../AndroidManifest.xml).
         What was missing was never the Service — it was these two permissions
         and the Dart-side AndroidSettings.foregroundNotificationConfig that
         tells it to go foreground. That wiring is in lib/her_position.dart.

         Both are load-bearing, not decorative: targetSdk is 36 (measured on
         the installed package), and from API 34 a location-typed foreground
         service that calls startForeground WITHOUT
         FOREGROUND_SERVICE_LOCATION throws SecurityException. FOREGROUND_SERVICE
         has been required since API 28.

         What she gets: a drive SHE starts (the "現在地を共有" button), a
         persistent notification she can see for as long as it runs, tapping it
         returns her to the app, and 停止 ends the drive AND the service
         (geolocator disables background mode when the last stream listener
         detaches — StreamHandlerImpl.java:155).

         ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION IS STILL WITHHELD. Unchanged, and not ours
         to move: no silent, notification-less background location. The service
         only ever runs behind a notification she started and can end. If a
         future change wants location without that notification, it is a
         different decision and belongs to the Chair, not to this file. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_LOCATION"/>

    <!-- POST_NOTIFICATIONS: the notification is the ONLY thing that makes the
         foreground service above honest, and without this permission it does
         not exist for her.

         MEASURED 2026-09-24 on AVD sng_arc (API 34, Android 14) with this app
         at targetSdk 36, BEFORE this line was added. The service started
         cleanly (dumpsys showed isForeground=true, types=00000008 for
         location, and no SecurityException) and the notification was BLOCKED:
         dumpsys notification gave numEnqueuedByApp=1, numPostedByApp=0,
         numBlocked=1, our package appeared 0 times in the Notification List,
         and the expanded shade (screenshot) held no entry from this app at all.
         The location pin appeared in the status bar. So the OS told her that
         SOMETHING was using her location, and nothing told her what it was,
         that we were watching the road for her, or how to stop it.

         That is the failure this permission exists to prevent, and it is worse
         than the one we set out to fix: a service running behind a notification
         she cannot see is the "silent, notification-less" collection that the
         comment above, the privacy policy and the 2026-07-10 removal all
         promised would never happen.

         Since API 33 an app targeting 33+ must hold this at RUNTIME; the
         declaration alone is not enough, which is why lib/main.dart asks for it
         before a drive starts and DOES NOT start the foreground service if she
         says no. Declared unconditionally: the OS ignores it below API 33. -->
    <!-- CT 2026-09-24, reconciling AAE's 3ce5de6 onto this line. AAE's version of
         this comment said the request was "OWED and needs a dependency decision".
         That is retired: the commit above wired the request as a first-party
         MethodChannel with NO new dependency, and made the degrade fail-closed.
         ONE fact from AAE's text is kept because it is not stated anywhere else:
         geolocator_android 4.6.2's own manifest declares only the Service, never
         this permission — so nothing upstream will ever supply it for us. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS"/>
    <application
        android:label="SNGNav"
        android:name="${applicationName}"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher">
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:theme="@style/LaunchTheme"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection|fontScale|screenLayout|density|uiMode"
            android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <!-- Specifies an Android theme to apply to this Activity as soon as
                 the Android process has started. This theme is visible to the user
                 while the Flutter UI initializes. After that, this theme continues
                 to determine the Window background behind the Flutter UI. -->
            <meta-data
              android:name="io.flutter.embedding.android.NormalTheme"
              android:resource="@style/NormalTheme"
              />
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
        <!-- BIS A-2: the commit this artifact was built from, stamped by
             gradle at build time. Read back via PackageManager
             (GET_META_DATA) so the app's identity comes from the installed
             package record rather than a compiled-in Dart constant. -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="dev.aki1770del.sngnav_app.gitSha"
            android:value="${gitSha}" />

        <!-- Don't delete the meta-data below.
             This is used by the Flutter tool to generate GeneratedPluginRegistrant.java -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="flutterEmbedding"
            android:value="2" />
    </application>
    <!-- Required to query activities that can process text, see:
         https://developer.android.com/training/package-visibility and
         https://developer.android.com/reference/android/content/Intent#ACTION_PROCESS_TEXT.

         In particular, this is used by the Flutter engine in io.flutter.plugin.text.ProcessTextPlugin. -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.PROCESS_TEXT"/>
            <data android:mimeType="text/plain"/>
        </intent>
        <!-- flutter_tts package-visibility requirement (Android 11+ /
             targetSdk >= 30): without this, the engine cannot see the
             system TTS service and speak() fails SILENTLY — the ja voice
             channel the driver relies on dies with no error surfaced (our speak
             call is inside a catch). Mandated by the flutter_tts README. -->
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.TTS_SERVICE"/>
        </intent>
    </queries>
</manifest>
